|~ `::call`, the step that calls a name, and what is declared under it:
   its constructor and the refusals of a call, a name that names
   nothing, a kind that answers no verb of the name, a value given
   modifiers, a call too deep and a failure of a host's implementation.
   ~|

:call
  |~~ The step of a command, `verb modifiers`: the name finds its verb
      through the kind of the subject and runs it with the modifiers.
      As a record, `:name` is the keyword of the name it calls and
      `:modifiers` the step of each modifier, when the call gives any.
      Like every record, a `::call` is the step its own text reads back
      as: the constructor checks the kind of each field, prints the step
      and reads it back. ::link~(::quote | doc)
      ::link~(::qlang/pipeline | doc)
  ~~|

:call/holdsTheNameAndTheModifiers
  |~~ A command is a call of its name and its modifiers. ~~|
  ::law~(~(filter ~(gt 1)) | payload | eq [::call{:name :filter :modifiers [~(gt 1)]}])

:call/readsTheName
  |~~ The name of the call is a keyword. ~~|
  ::law~(~(count) | first | /name | eq :count)

:call/buildsAStep
  |~~ A map under `::call` builds the step. ~~|
  ::law~({:name :filter :modifiers [~(gt 1)]} | tag ::call | [/] | tag ::quote | eq ~(filter ~(gt 1)))

:call/construct
  |~~ The step of a command from its record, `:name` and `:modifiers`,
      which reads back as itself.
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:call/PayloadNotSchemaError
  |~~ A `::call` is a map of `:name`, a keyword, and `:modifiers`, a
      vector of steps, and `:field` names the field that does not fit:
      `{:name :take :modifiers [2]} | tag ::call` is the step `take 2`.
      ::link~(::call | doc)
  ~~|

:call/PayloadNotSchemaError/refusesANameThatIsNoKeyword
  |~~ A `:name` that is no keyword is refused, `:field` naming it. ~~|
  ::law~(::call{:name 42} !| [type /field] | eq [::call/PayloadNotSchemaError :name])

:call/PayloadNotSchemaError/buildsTheStepOfACall
  |~~ A keyword and a vector of steps build the step of the call. ~~|
  ::law~({:name :take :modifiers [2]} | tag ::call | [/] | tag ::quote | eq ~(take 2))

:call/ReadBackDiffersError
  |~~ The `::call` prints as text that reads back as another step, or as
      none, and `:printed` is that text: a name of two words, `:"a b"`,
      prints as two calls, a name that reads as a number, `:"1"`, as the
      number, and an empty vector of modifiers as a call that gives none.
      The name of a call is one word, `:count`, and a call that gives no
      modifiers holds no `:modifiers`.
      ::link~(::call | doc)
  ~~|

:call/ReadBackDiffersError/refusesANameOfTwoWords
  |~~ A name with a space prints as two words, which read back as another step. ~~|
  ::law~(::call{:name :"a b"} !| [type /printed] | eq [::call/ReadBackDiffersError "a b"])

:call/ReadBackDiffersError/refusesANameThatReadsAsANumber
  |~~ A name that reads as a number prints as the number. ~~|
  ::law~(::call{:name :"1"} !| [type /printed] | eq [::call/ReadBackDiffersError "1"])

:call/ReadBackDiffersError/refusesAnEmptyVectorOfModifiers
  |~~ An empty vector of modifiers prints as a call that gives none. ~~|
  ::law~(::call{:name :count :modifiers []}
    !| [type /printed] | eq [::call/ReadBackDiffersError "count"])

:call/HostFailureError
  |~~ A failure of the host's implementation: an error of the language
      the runtime is written in escaped a verb, which is a defect of
      that verb's host. `:message` carries the host's account of it and
      `:causes` the accounts of the failures beneath it, the facts every
      host can give, so a port of the runtime to another language
      changes no fact a query reads; the stack, the class and the fields
      of the host's error stay with the host. The step and the input
      are those of any error; recovery is a `!|` step, and the repair
      belongs to the host, which declares a refusal of its own where it
      has facts to hand.
      ::link~(::call | doc)
  ~~|

:call/HostFailureError/message
  |~~ The host's account of the failure. ~~|
  ::string

:call/HostFailureError/causes
  |~~ The accounts of the failures beneath it, outermost first. ~~|
  ::vec

:call/ValueTakesNoModifiersError
  |~~ A name the scope binds to a value takes no modifiers: the value is
      read bare, `:five 5 | five`, and `:name` holds the name. A word
      declared under a name a value of the scope already holds is
      shadowed by the value, `:addresses` naming where the word lives, and
      a call by its address reaches it, `any/five`.
      ::link~(::call | doc)
  ~~|

:call/ValueTakesNoModifiersError/name
  |~~ The name, which holds a value and no word. ~~|
  ::keyword

:call/ValueTakesNoModifiersError/addresses
  |~~ The addresses where a word of that name lives, one a declaration shadows among them. ~~|
  ::set

:call/ValueTakesNoModifiersError/refusesModifiersOnAValue
  |~~ A name that holds a value refuses the modifiers a call gives it. ~~|
  ::law~(5 | :five / | five 42
    !| [type /name /actualType] | eq [::call/ValueTakesNoModifiersError :five ::number])

:call/ValueTakesNoModifiersError/namesTheVerbTheValueShadows
  |~~ The refusal names where the word the value shadows lives. ~~|
  ::law~(:five 5 | :any/five ~(add 5) | 3 | five 42 !| /addresses | eq #[::any/five])

:call/ValueTakesNoModifiersError/reachesTheShadowedWordByItsAddress
  |~~ A call by its address reaches the word a value shadows. ~~|
  ::law~(:five 5 | :any/five ~(add 5) | 3 | any/five | eq 8)

:call/KindAnswersNoVerbError
  |~~ The kind of the subject has no verb of the name while other kinds
      have one: `:name` holds the name, `:actualType` the kind of the
      subject, and `:addresses` the verbs of the name and the kinds they
      serve. The subject is turned into one of those kinds first, a soft
      read that answered null given its fallback,
      `at :items | coalesce [] | count`, or a verb of another name was
      meant.
      ::link~(::call | doc) ::link~(::any/coalesce | doc)
  ~~|

:call/KindAnswersNoVerbError/name
  |~~ The word the step called. ~~|
  ::keyword

:call/KindAnswersNoVerbError/namesTheKindAndTheVerbs
  |~~ The refusal names the kind of the subject and where the verb lives. ~~|
  ::law~(1 | count !| [/actualType (/addresses | has ::vec/count)] | eq [::number true])

:call/KindAnswersNoVerbError/listsEveryKindTheVerbServes
  |~~ `:addresses` lists every kind the verb serves. ~~|
  ::law~(5 | count
    !| [type /addresses]
    |  eq [::call/KindAnswersNoVerbError #[::map/count ::set/count ::string/count ::vec/count]])

:call/KindAnswersNoVerbError/namesTheWordsOfAReadersNouns
  |~~ The words of the name under a reader's nouns are named by their addresses. ~~|
  ::law~(:Box |~~ A box. ~~| | :Ring |~~ A ring. ~~|
    | :Box/area ~(1) | :Ring/area ~(2)
    | 5 | area
    !| [type /addresses]
    |  eq [::call/KindAnswersNoVerbError #[::Box/area ::Ring/area]])

:call/KindAnswersNoVerbError/turnsTheSubjectFirst
  |~~ A soft read that answered null is given its fallback first. ~~|
  ::law~({} | at :items | coalesce [] | count | eq 0)

:call/UnknownNameError
  |~~ No name of the scope and no verb of any kind answers the name:
      `:name` holds the name as typed, and `:nearest` the names it was
      likely meant to be, those within an edit distance of a third of its
      length, one at least, and of those the nearest alone, in the order
      of their spelling; an edit is an insertion, a deletion, a
      substitution or a swap of two neighbours, and a name with nothing
      near holds `[]`. `:addresses` holds none, since a name that the
      verbs of other kinds answer is refused by
      `::call/KindAnswersNoVerbError`, which names them. A name is
      declared before the steps that read it, `:x 5 | x`, and a fact of a
      record is no word but a key, read by projection, `/name`.
      ::link~(::call | doc) ::link~(::call/KindAnswersNoVerbError | doc)
  ~~|

:call/UnknownNameError/name
  |~~ The name the step called. ~~|
  ::keyword

:call/UnknownNameError/refusesANameNothingDeclares
  |~~ A name nothing declares is refused, with no addresses. ~~|
  ::law~(thisIsNotDefined !| [type /addresses] | eq [::call/UnknownNameError #[]])

:call/UnknownNameError/suggestsTheNearestName
  |~~ The names nearest in spelling are suggested. ~~|
  ::law~(fist !| /nearest | eq [:first])

:call/UnknownNameError/suggestsTheReadersNames
  |~~ A reader's own names are suggested too. ~~|
  ::law~(:limit 3 | limti !| /nearest | eq [:limit])

:call/UnknownNameError/suggestsNothingFarAway
  |~~ A name near nothing suggests nothing. ~~|
  ::law~(zzzzqqq !| /nearest | eq [])

:call/UnknownNameError/refusesANameReadBeforeItsDeclaration
  |~~ A name read before its declaration names nothing yet. ~~|
  ::law~(x | :x 5 !| type | eq ::call/UnknownNameError)

:call/UnknownNameError/readsNoFactAsAWord
  |~~ A fact of a record called as a word names nothing. ~~|
  ::law~(~(take 2) | first | name !| [type /name] | eq [::call/UnknownNameError :name])

:call/UnknownAddressError
  |~~ A name with a path names nothing: no declaration wrote that
      address. `:address` holds the address as a tag name, which the
      axes read, and `:nearest` the addresses it was likely meant to be,
      and `manifest` lists the names a noun holds, `::vec | manifest`. An
      address that names a value reads it, as a bare name does.
      ::link~(::call | doc) ::link~(::tag/manifest | doc)
  ~~|

:call/UnknownAddressError/address
  |~~ The address the step called. ~~|
  ::tag

:call/UnknownAddressError/nearest
  |~~ The addresses nearest to it in spelling. ~~|
  ::vec

:call/UnknownAddressError/refusesAnAddressNothingDeclares
  |~~ An address no declaration wrote is refused. ~~|
  ::law~([1 2 3] | vec/nothing !| [type /address] | eq [::call/UnknownAddressError ::vec/nothing])

:call/UnknownAddressError/suggestsTheNearestAddress
  |~~ The addresses nearest in spelling are suggested. ~~|
  ::law~([1] | vec/countt !| /nearest | has ::vec/count)

:call/UnknownAddressError/readsAValueByItsAddress
  |~~ A value under a label is read by its address. ~~|
  ::law~(:Box |~~ A box. ~~| | :Box/side 3 | Box/side | eq 3)

:call/EvaluationDepthExceededError
  |~~ A call too deep: every verb body, modifier, `apply` and
      constructor of a tag runs one frame below the step that called it,
      and the frame past the budget refuses, `:depth` the refused frame
      and `:limit` the budget. A word that calls itself without a base
      case is refused here, on the fail track, while the host's memory
      stays bounded: the case that ends the recursion is tested first,
      `:any/down ~(if (eq 0) ~(0) ~(sub 1 | down))`.
      ::link~(::call | doc) ::link~(::any/if | doc)
  ~~|

:call/EvaluationDepthExceededError/depth
  |~~ The frame the budget refused. ~~|
  ::number

:call/EvaluationDepthExceededError/limit
  |~~ The budget, the frames one evaluation may nest. ~~|
  ::number

:call/EvaluationDepthExceededError/refusesARunawayRecursion
  |~~ A word that calls itself without end is refused past the budget. ~~|
  ::law~(:any/inf ~(add 1 | inf) | 0 | inf !| type | eq ::call/EvaluationDepthExceededError)

:call/EvaluationDepthExceededError/namesADepthPastTheLimit
  |~~ The depth refused lies past the limit. ~~|
  ::law~(:q ~(apply q) | apply q !| :e / | /depth | gt (e | /limit))

:call/EvaluationDepthExceededError/endsARecursionByItsBaseCase
  |~~ A recursion that tests its base case first ends. ~~|
  ::law~(:any/down ~(if (eq 0) ~(0) ~(sub 1 | down)) | 5 | down | eq 0)
